Fitting a Second-Order Model

# Create second lag
dat_order2 &lt;- dat |&gt;
  group_by(id) |&gt;
  mutate(
    y_lag1 = lag(y, 1),
    y_lag2 = lag(y, 2)
  ) |&gt;
  ungroup() |&gt;
  filter(!is.na(y_lag2))

fit_order2 &lt;- glm(y ~ y_lag1 + y_lag2 + trt + time,
                  data = dat_order2,
                  family = binomial)

summary(fit_order2)$coefficients

SAS Reference: ML under MAR (PROC MIXED)
The likelihood-based MAR analysis (our primary approach) in SAS. PROC MIXED with REML uses all available occasions, so it is valid under MAR if the mean and covariance are correct (no imputation needed).
/* Likelihood-based analysis valid under MAR (data in long form) */
proc mixed data = long method = reml;
  class id;
  model y = time / solution;
  repeated time / subject = id type = un;   /* unstructured covariance */
run;
For the multiple-imputation thread (a Chapter 18 preview), the SAS pipeline is PROC MI then PROC MIANALYZE:
proc mi data = wide nimpute = 20 seed = 667 out = imp;
  var y1 y2 y3 y4 y5;                        /* monotone or MCMC imputation */
run;
proc mixed data = imp; by _Imputation_;
  model y = time / solution covb;
  ods output SolutionF = mixparms;
run;
proc mianalyze parms = mixparms;            /* Rubin's rules pooling */
  modeleffects intercept time;
run;

This is the static SAS-equivalent reference slide the spec requires for a reference deck (not executed). Two points to make. (1) PROC MIXED with type=un and method=reml is the SAS twin of our gls/lme MAR analysis: it maximizes the observed-data likelihood and uses every recorded occasion, so it is the right primary tool under MAR with a flexible covariance. (2) The PROC MI / PROC MIANALYZE block is the SAS multiple-imputation pipeline; flag it as a Chapter 18 preview, since proper imputation modeling and Rubin’s-rules pooling are covered there. PROC MI defaults to a monotone method when the pattern is monotone (dropout) and MCMC otherwise. Keep the seed (667) for reproducibility, mirroring our R convention.

SAS Equivalent (reference only)
FLW Tables 14.2/14.6 use PROC GLIMMIX with adaptive quadrature. The standalone program for our random-slope Poisson GLMM:
proc glimmix data=epilepsy method=quad(qpoints=50);
  class id;
  model seizures = trt|visit_c log_base age_c
        / dist=poisson link=log solution;
  random intercept visit_c / subject=id type=un;
run;






Note


trt|visit_c expands to the main effects and their interaction. type=un gives the unstructured \(2\times2\) \(G\) (the correlated form); use type=vc for the uncorrelated version. method=quad(qpoints=50) requests 50-point adaptive Gaussian quadrature, matching FLW; /solution prints the fixed-effect estimates.




This is the static, non-runnable SAS slide the spec requires, a complete standalone program (not a fragment table). Walk the mapping to our glmer call: CLASS id is the grouping factor, MODEL is the fixed part with DIST/LINK, RANDOM intercept visit_c / TYPE=UN is (1 + visit_c | id). method=quad(qpoints=50) is the FLW 50-point AGQ; note SAS GLIMMIX can do AGQ for q=2, unlike lme4. Shown for reference only; students run R.

Compare Coefficient Estimates

extract_coefs &lt;- function(fit, name) {
  coef(summary(fit)) |&gt;
    as.data.frame() |&gt;
    tibble::rownames_to_column("term") |&gt;
    mutate(model = name) |&gt;
    select(model, term, Estimate, `Std.err`)
}

bind_rows(
  extract_coefs(gee_ind, "Independence"),
  extract_coefs(gee_exch, "Exchangeable"),
  extract_coefs(gee_ar1, "AR(1)"),
  extract_coefs(gee_unstr, "Unstructured")
) |&gt;
  filter(term == "trtProgabide") |&gt;
  knitr::kable(digits = 4)

